Z||000000||kornholio||01-03-2001||09:33 AM||||Hi,<BR>Is there anyway I can test a relay with a voltmeter?  Thanks.||||reg||1
Z||000001||FieroJoe||01-03-2001||10:13 AM||||you'd have to test for continuity, unless you have voltage already going to the relay.  If youve already got power to it, just see if there is power (volts) coming out the other side of the coil||||reg||1
Z||000002||hugh||01-03-2001||06:44 PM||||A relay is nothing more than a magnetic coil,an armature(moving contact)and a stationary contact.You can check the coil by putting your meter or test light on one of the coil ends and then the other.If you have voltage on both ends,there is no ground.I think most,if not all manufacturers control the ground wires on relays with switches related to the relay's function.On any relay you should have at least 2 hot wires,one to supply the coil circuit and one to be controlled.When the relay closes a third wire should get hot electrically to supply whatever item is being controlled.Of course this needs to be done with the key on.I hope I wasn't too confusing.    ||||reg||1
